  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 140 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThunderstorms likely across much of the area Thursday evening\nthen most activity ends toward midnight, as weak subsidence \nspreads across srn CO. On Friday, mid-level flow takes on a \nmore wly component, as western U.S. upper trough moves into the \nnrn Rockies. Mountains will see widespread storms once again Fri\nafternoon/evening, with heavy rain potential perhaps shifting a\nlittle eastward into the ern mountains by evening. Weak cold \nfront on the plains by Fri evening may also help enhance \nconvective chances on the plains and I-25 corridor. Upper low \nthen closes off over WY on Sat, with trough axis into wrn CO by\nSat night. Mountains along the Continental Divide may see some \ndrying as main moist plume is pushed eastward, though still \nenough residual moisture around for at least isolated storms. \nEastern plains may become active late Sat afternoon, as cold \nfront drops south, bringing an enhanced surge of usplope \nmoisture/instability behind it. Best forcing may be just north \nof the area in nern CO, though still expect sct/numerous showers\nand storms across the eastern mountains/plains, especially from\nlate Sat afternoon well into the evening. Cooler on Sunday, \nwith much less in the way of convection over the mountains as \ndrier air spreads in from the west. Still a question if post-\nfrontal upslope and instability will be enough to produce \nshowers/storms over the eastern mountains and plains Sun/Mon \nafternoon/evening, as at least a few models/ensemble members \nshow a drier solution. Current NBM forecast leans wet, so still \na good deal of uncertainty in precip chances as we head into \nnext week. A little more confidence in the temperatures \nforecast, which suggests readings slightly cooler than seasonal \naverages from Sunday into Tuesday.",
